{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792268744,"nonce":"n+DRkE9JErv1eNkDmxQz7Ut4cxE=","message":"Fixed in af80bcd0792d3292976284eebb004b5eb87d60af. Cause: Dynamic float comparison treated NaN as an ordered value. hlp_dyn_compare (used by compiled code) returned 0, 'equal', for any pair involving NaN, and returned equal for an identical box before looking at it, so a Watch comparing the old and new value of a NaN signal thought nothing had changed. The interpreter had the matching defects: a shared box or identical raw bits counted as equal, and NaN ordered as 'equal', so nan \u003c= x and nan \u003e= x were true.\n\nNow NaN equals nothing, itself included, and satisfies no ordering test, as in Haxe's evaluator: hlp_dyn_compare answers the invalid-comparison sentinel for it, the two Cranelift lowerings and the LLVM one refuse the sentinel in \u003c, \u003c=, \u003e and \u003e=, and the interpreter treats it as unordered. ashui's preserved reproducer (nan-watch-repro.hl) went from 127 missed updates in hybrid and 200 in jit to 0 in interp, hybrid and jit; the fixture TestDynNaN (== != \u003c \u003c= \u003e \u003e= over NaN/NaN, NaN against floats and ints, in both orders) matches Haxe eval in interp, hybrid (threshold 1), jit and the LLVM tier. Note for the record: stock HashLink does NOT do this (it reports NaN == NaN as true for Dynamic floats); ash follows Haxe's semantics.","files":null}]}